Excellent point! You're absolutely right. The updates to `left` and `right` still shrink the range each time, so the loop cannot loop forever.

Here's a revised version of the code that includes this modification:
```javascript
var search = function(nums, target) {
    if (nums.length === 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    let left = 0;
    let right = nums.length - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);

        if (nums[mid] === target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (nums[mid] < target) {
            left = mid + 1;
        } else {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    if (left === right && nums[left] === target) {
        return left;
    }

    return -1;
};
```
With this modification, the loop will stop when `left` and `right` are the same index, which is the correct behavior for this function.

